  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =6x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =6x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3{x}^{2}+1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 1 \\\\ u & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(u){|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(4)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
